WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.909
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.209 --> 00:00:08.317
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.617 --> 00:00:14.764
Unit twelve: Migration. In this unit, you will discuss the causes and effects of migration.

4
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.332
economic migrant.

5
00:00:18.032 --> 00:00:22.527
economic migrant means a person who moves to another country to find work.

6
00:00:22.827 --> 00:00:26.197
Many economic migrants from Cambodia work in Thailand.

7
00:00:26.897 --> 00:00:30.223
Your turn. Say: economic migrant.

8
00:00:32.923 --> 00:00:34.411
economic migrant.

9
00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:36.522
remittance.

10
00:00:37.222 --> 00:00:40.824
remittance means money sent home by people working abroad.

11
00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:44.064
Remittances support thousands of rural families.

12
00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:48.206
Your turn. Say: remittance.

13
00:00:50.906 --> 00:00:51.860
remittance.

14
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:53.979
brain drain.

15
00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:57.979
brain drain means the emigration of highly skilled people.

16
00:00:58.279 --> 00:01:01.451
The brain drain has left hospitals short of doctors.

17
00:01:02.151 --> 00:01:04.513
Your turn. Say: brain drain.

18
00:01:07.213 --> 00:01:08.144
brain drain.

19
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.581
asylum seeker.

20
00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:16.101
asylum seeker means a person who asks for protection in another country.

21
00:01:16.401 --> 00:01:19.411
The asylum seeker waited two years for a decision.

22
00:01:20.111 --> 00:01:23.820
Your turn. Say: asylum seeker.

23
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.881
asylum seeker.

24
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:30.523
integration policy.

25
00:01:31.223 --> 00:01:35.986
integration policy means government plans to help migrants join society.

26
00:01:36.286 --> 00:01:39.504
Language classes are part of the integration policy.

27
00:01:40.204 --> 00:01:43.913
Your turn. Say: integration policy.

28
00:01:46.613 --> 00:01:48.404
integration policy.

29
00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:50.627
border control.

30
00:01:51.327 --> 00:01:55.497
border control means checks on people and goods entering a country.

31
00:01:55.797 --> 00:01:59.504
Border control has become stricter since the pandemic.

32
00:02:00.204 --> 00:02:03.355
Your turn. Say: border control.

33
00:02:06.055 --> 00:02:07.335
border control.

34
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.699
labour migration.

35
00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:13.676
labour migration means moving abroad for employment.

36
00:02:13.976 --> 00:02:19.099
Labour migration to South Korea is popular among young Cambodians.

37
00:02:19.799 --> 00:02:23.078
Your turn. Say: labour migration.

38
00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.116
labour migration.

39
00:02:28.117 --> 00:02:29.373
undocumented.

40
00:02:30.073 --> 00:02:34.766
undocumented means without official permission to live or work in a country.

41
00:02:35.066 --> 00:02:38.331
Undocumented workers often face exploitation.

42
00:02:39.031 --> 00:02:42.450
Your turn. Say: undocumented.

43
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.279
undocumented.

44
00:02:47.283 --> 00:02:49.469
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:53.814
My uncle works in Korea. He sends remittances every month.

46
00:02:55.117 --> 00:02:57.990
Many families do. Does he want to come back?

47
00:02:59.283 --> 00:03:02.714
He hopes to, eventually. My aunt doesn't, though.

48
00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.351
Labour migration brings income, but also costs.

49
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.266
Such as the brain drain?

50
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.921
That's one. Skilled workers leave, and fewer return than expected.

51
00:03:17.217 --> 00:03:19.123
And undocumented workers?

52
00:03:20.417 --> 00:03:26.575
They're at greater risk. Fair integration policies could help, and so could safer legal routes.

53
00:03:27.883 --> 00:03:31.740
Grammar focus: Ellipsis and substitution.

54
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.909
Ellipsis means leaving out words that are already understood.

55
00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:41.204
Substitution replaces them with words like so, do, one or that.

56
00:03:41.504 --> 00:03:46.411
For example: Does he want to come back? He hopes to. My aunt doesn't, though.

57
00:03:46.711 --> 00:03:52.357
Ellipsis and substitution avoid repetition and make speech sound natural and fluent.

58
00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:57.094
Now read and listen. Leaving Home to Support Home.

59
00:03:57.394 --> 00:04:00.903
Over one million Cambodians are estimated to work abroad.

60
00:04:02.217 --> 00:04:08.013
Most are economic migrants seeking better wages in Thailand, Korea or Malaysia.

61
00:04:09.317 --> 00:04:14.648
The remittances they send home pay for school fees, healthcare and new houses.

62
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:20.213
Some migrants return with savings and skills; others never do.

63
00:04:21.517 --> 00:04:26.895
Highly educated migrants are less likely to return, which contributes to brain drain.

64
00:04:28.183 --> 00:04:34.037
Undocumented workers face particular risks, including low pay and unsafe conditions.

65
00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:40.380
Governments on both sides are trying to create safer, legal migration routes.

66
00:04:41.683 --> 00:04:46.690
Migration, then, is neither simply good nor simply bad; it is both.

67
00:04:47.983 --> 00:04:50.636
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:50.936 --> 00:04:53.945
Money sent home by people working abroad is blank.

69
00:04:54.245 --> 00:04:59.013
A, remittance. B, border control. C, brain drain.

70
00:05:03.017 --> 00:05:05.794
The answer is A: remittance.

71
00:05:07.283 --> 00:05:10.031
'Are you coming tonight?' 'I hope blank.'

72
00:05:10.331 --> 00:05:13.067
A, it. B, so. C, that.

73
00:05:17.083 --> 00:05:19.211
The answer is B: so.

74
00:05:20.717 --> 00:05:23.494
She likes coffee, and blank her brother.

75
00:05:23.794 --> 00:05:28.179
A, so does. B, so is. C, does so.

76
00:05:32.183 --> 00:05:34.589
The answer is A: so does.

77
00:05:36.083 --> 00:05:39.035
The emigration of highly skilled people is blank.

78
00:05:39.335 --> 00:05:44.637
A, labour migration. B, brain drain. C, integration policy.

79
00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.091
The answer is B: brain drain.

80
00:05:52.583 --> 00:05:56.023
According to the text, who is less likely to return?

81
00:05:56.323 --> 00:06:01.567
A, undocumented workers. B, highly educated migrants. C, farmers.

82
00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:08.908
The answer is B: highly educated migrants.

83
00:06:10.417 --> 00:06:15.983
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is labour migration good or bad for Cambodia?

84
00:06:21.283 --> 00:06:27.544
Part two: Weigh up two benefits and two drawbacks, then give your conclusion.

85
00:06:33.844 --> 00:06:49.560
It brings real benefits: remittances reduce poverty, and returning workers bring skills. However, families are separated, and skilled people may not return. Overall, I think it helps, but only if migration is safe and legal.

86
00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.277
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:53.283 --> 00:06:58.676
Great work today! You learned eight new words and ellipsis and substitution.

88
00:06:58.976 --> 00:07:02.708
Today's value is empathy. Think about how others feel.

89
00:07:03.008 --> 00:07:06.055
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:06.355 --> 00:07:08.961
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

